From: Freekids@aol.com
Date: Mon, 28 Nov 1994 18:08:25 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-471] Extropian schools

> Extropian schools

The schools that appear to be most consistent with Extropian
values are Sudbury Valley School, Framingham, MA, and the schools modeled on
it (currently in CA, OR, UT, ME, WV, CT, and PA).  In the words of Sudbury
Valley's catalogue:

The fundamental premises of the school are simple: that all
people are curious by nature; that the most efficient, long-
lasting, and profound learning takes place when started and
pursued by the learner; that all people are creative if they are
allowed to develop their unique talents; that age-mixing among
students promotes growth in all members of the group; and that
freedom is essential to the development of personal
responsibility. . . .  In practice this means that students
initiate all their own activities and create their own
environments.  The physical plant, the staff, and the equipment
are there for the students to use as the need arises. 

I am part of a group starting a Sudbury Valley-type school in
Santa Clara, CA.  Santa Clara Valley School will open in fall
1995 and will enroll students aged 5-19.  I know of about 10
other startups.

> Montessori schools

The essence of the Sudbury Valley approach is the complete
freedom of students to structure their time and activities as
they see fit, without intervention, coercion, or restraint by
others.  Montessori and other progressive approaches do give
students some freedom, but far less.  Adults still largely decide
what, when, and how students "must" learn.

> Profit potential

Most Sudbury Valley devotees are committed capitalists.  However,
the schools are all nonprofits, and rewards (to date) come in the form of
community, not cash.  

For books, tapes etc. about the Sudbury Valley approach to
education, call the school at (508) 877-3030.  For information
about Santa Clara Valley School, call Stuart Williams-Ley at (408)
243-9035 or e-mail Freekids@aol.com.


