From: Ryan Derek <DEREK@alcor.org>
Date:  Mon, 28 Nov 94 16:19:01 
Subject: [#94-11-472] Alcor in Phoenix (was: POLL: safe living)

Craig Presson writes:

>The bright white light of stupidity goes off in my head. *Of 
>course* the one group who would do this analysis would be 
>cryonicists.

I detect a compliment in there somewhere.  Thank you.

>And of course the coincidence of the name "Phoenix" was just an
>add-on, right? Uh-huh.

Well, we didn't name the city, and we didn't make it the safest 
place (in terms of natural disaster risk) in the U.S., but we are quite 
happy with the coincidence.  In fact, we named our members' 
newsletter (which we just started this year) _The Alcor Phoenix_.

More Alcor/phoenix trivia for the interested:

We had to rework our logo because of its strong resemblance to the 
city of Phoenix's logo.  Like the old one, the new logo bears a 
simple silhouette of a phoenix (i.e., bird and flames.)  The change 
(an improvement in both appearance and conceptual depth, we like 
to think) is that the new one also contains a reversed out silhouette 
of a man rising inside the phoenix.  ExI (and Alcor) director Ralph 
Whelan is the being most responsible for the new art.  The 
exceptional biceps on the man inside the phoenix were provided 
(i.e., modeled for) by Tom Saquet, who takes great pleasure in this 
bit of notoriety.

Though there's really no way for us to know when it might be 
possible to revive today's cryonics patients, Phoenix would be a 
great place for it to happen.  I can just imagine the folklore that 
might be invented about the name.  If I were writing a sci fi novel 
about revival, I'd be hard pressed to come up with a better city 
name--fictional or otherwise--for the story's setting.

Though come to think of it, "Extropolis" might also be nice.

Derek Ryan
derek@alcor.org

