From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Mon, 28 Nov 94 22:27:38 EST
Subject: [#94-11-476] NANOTECH: Interstellar travel (was Drexler on self-replication)

Ed Regis says:

>So far as I'm aware, exponential growth is not a necessary element of
>Drexler's nanotechnology.  When Drexler talks about it in Engines (p. 58,
>paperback edition), the prospect of regularly-doubling replicators is
>portrayed as a threat, not as something that's desirable.  

Uncontrolled replication is undesirable but consider one of the most
important applications of nanotech discussed in Engines of Creation -
interstellar travel (pg 260-261 paperback) 

... But nanotechnology will allow a different approach to accelerating 
lightsails and stopping their cargo. Replicating assemblers will make it
easy to build large lasers, lenses and sails. ... 
  In flight, computer-driven assembler systems aboard the sail (powered by
yet more laser light from the point of departure) could rebuild the sail
into a long, thin traveling-wave accelerator. This can be used to
electrically accelerate a hollow shell of strong material several microns in
radius and containing about a cubic micron of cargo;...As the accelerator
plunges through the target star system, it fires backward at a speed chosen
to leave the cargo almost at rest....
  The residual velocity of the projectile can be directed to make it strike
the atmosphere of a Mars- or Venus-like planet... The cargo consisting of an
assembler and nanocomputer system, can then use the light of the local sun
and local carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, and oxygen (likely to be found in any
planetary atmosphere) to replicate and build larger structures.
  An early project would be construction of a receiver for further
instructions from home, including plans for complex devices.  These can
include rockets able to get off the planet (used as a target chiefly for its
atmospheric cushion) to reach a better location for construction work.  The
resulting system of replicating assemblers could build virtually anything,
including intelligent systems for exploration.  To solve the lightsail
stopping problem for the massive passenger vehicles that might follow, the
system could build an array of braking lasers as large as the launching
lasers back home.  The construction could be completed in a matter of weeks
following delivery of the cubic micron ``seed.'' This system illustrates one
way to spread human civilization to the stars at only slightly less than the
speed of light.

End quote

Building large systems of lasers from a cubic micron seed in a matter of
weeks demands rapid replication. This task is no doubt best attempted with a
mature knowledge of nanotech. Does the replication have to occur at an
exponential rate?  Well, a carefully designed replicator can expand at a
subexponential growth rate if desired, eg, linear, quadratic, cubic, etc.
Also, a replicator is restricted by the feedstocks available which must be
incorporated into new replicators and which should not be exhausted.  But
there is a whole sun system to explore and ultimately a whole universe to
explore. Exponential growth for an appropriate duration makes sense to me
for this application.  

Also, one can recognize that replicators can build variant replicators which
can then build other variant replicators, so that one can ask if what is
happening is really replication.  Also, one can note that the instructions
being transmitted from home need note be embedded in the objects being
constructed so one can ask if this is really replication.  These
distinctions should not be ignored but also should not be belabored in my
opinion.

Tipler in The Physics of Immortality (pg 59-60) talks about self-reproducing
intelligent machines.  He discusses schemes which have payloads of 100 grams
for interstellar probes and Tipler invokes Drexler nanotech to explain why
this payload size is adequate.
    Gregory Sullivan

