From: hhuang@MIT.EDU
Date: Tue, 29 Nov 1994 00:17:55 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-478] FEMMES: Paglia and Madonna

On Camille Paglia, Craig Presson writes,

>If [Paglia] shocks and provokes her way to a mass audience with the message
>that leftist feminism is off the track, and that the humanities in
>academia have turned into a stinking cesspool, and that it's OK for
>women to use their sexual power for their own ends, while taking 
>full responsibility, then *hey* I want to see her tour with Madonna,
>OK? M. would be the first tramp steamer to have a philosopher as an
>opening act.

	Replace "Camille Paglia" with "Rush Limbaugh."  Suppose Rush
Limbaugh shocks his way to a mass audience with the message that
liberalism is way off track, that PC'ism has turned the humanities in
academia into a stinking cesspool, and that men should use their
sexual power for their own ends (e.g., perpetuating sexism).
	In fact, Rush Limbaugh does all of the above.
	In terms of damaging left-liberalism and radical feminism, I'd
say Limbaugh outdoes Paglia.  On balance, I find his existence, just
like Camille Paglia's, a good thing.  But Limbaugh, or Paglia, as an
extropian icon?  I say that's going too far.

On Madonna, Goeff Dale writes,

>Madonna on the other hand, is an extropian heroine. She's made millions
>getting people to see things in a different, more sex-positive way. She's
>brought aspects of the gay culture into the mainstream. And she's done it
>all while making few comprimises.

	While there is no one notable pornography actor or actress, as
an entity the pornographic industry has made many millions in getting
people to see things in a decidedly more ... sex-explicit way.  The
industry has certainly brought more aspects of gay culture into the
populace (witness men-men porn aimed at gay men; women-women porn
targeted primarily at straight men).
	In terms of bringing sex of all types into the culture, and in
terms of frustrating prudery and censorship, I say the pornography
industry outdoes Madonna.  On balance, I find its existence, just like
Madonna's, a good thing.  But the pornographic industry, or Madonna,
as an extropian icon?  Again, I say that's going too far.

	So you can't just evaluate a person based upon their influence
on society.  Personal taste matters, too.  As for me, I think Madonna
sings nice songs (sometimes), and Paglia says neat things (sometimes)
-- but it's not enough to overcome their flaws.

-Han Y. Huang                                         hhuang@mit.edu

